! Silicon, eight atoms, run as noncollinear spinors -- the cost of npol = 2.
!
! Physically identical to si8-1k.in: no spin-orbit coupling, no magnetization, so
! the answer is the same to machine precision and every eigenvalue is doubled.
! That is exactly what makes it a measurement rather than a benchmark of a
! different calculation -- the pair says what a spinor wavefunction costs, with
! nothing else varying. Smearing is added to both sides of the pair when the
! comparison is run, since a spinor band holds one electron and 'fixed' would be
! filling a different number of bands in the two runs.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav=1, celldm(1) =10.20,
    nat=8, ntyp=1,
    ecutwfc=12.0
    noncolin = .true.
    occupations='smearing', smearing='gaussian', degauss=0.02
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.00 0.50 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.00 0.50
 Si 0.50 0.50 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
 Si 0.25 0.75 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.25 0.75
 Si 0.75 0.75 0.25
K_POINTS
  1
   0.250000  0.250000  0.250000   1.00
